g more often for some groups), each a case told by what happened, two views, a dated source, no company named; retrieval of grade 6's design-for-users judgment. Session 40: why such biases happen, in simple terms, linked to session 37's training data; is the outcome fair and inclusive? Session 41 (society): a short claim for a new case: who is helped, who is hurt, and why. Out: how training works inside a model, neural networks, changing a model other than through its examples (MS-ALG-ML-07 is met by testing, reading results and improving the data), global AI policy, probability numbers (math grade 7 unit 8), a live generated-text tool. End-of-unit (day 42): justify an approach for two new problems (MS-ALG-PS-04); read a new model's test results, name the missing examples and the improvement, and say how to check it worked (MS-ALG-ML-07); judge a new daily algorithm's benefit, harm and likely bias (MS-ALG-IM-10); respond day 43. Tools: a picture-sorter model in the page (train, test, show accuracy; job: test and improve a training set; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md). Curio Class writes: the recycling picture sets and test set, the four-problem cards, the bias case cards with their sources, the sorting-card stand-in | After unit 3 because the training set is a data-table and bias is a data choice; MS-ALG-ML-06 (grade 6) comes before ML-07 and DC-21 to 24 (grade 6) before it (STANDARD.md ordering). Choosing an approach opens the unit so a learned method is first seen as one choice among several. Our own: the archived Year 8 and key stage 3 have no machine learning unit | Candidate: kept, a second retrain round on session 38 for pairs that finish, zero added days | Bias cases touch race, accent and disability: talk stays on how a system was built, tested and used, never on a group or a classmate; no student's face or voice is used. The board-game example replaces a fee-waiver example so no case turns on family income (Row changes 9). Agent reads: sensitivity (the starting 