it. Session 8: revise the design after the partner's trace and write how it works. Out: procedures with parameters or return values (grade 9), debugging and testing as scored skills (unit 2's MS-PRO-TR-19), lists and other data structures (MS-ALG-PS-01 does not expect them; lists come in unit 2), and no score on whether the built program runs: MS-ALG-PS-01 is met by the design and its trace. End-of-unit (day 9): mark the roles of loop, choice, variables and procedures in a new twenty-line program and predict its output for two inputs (MS-PRO-RD-17); design a short algorithm for a new problem with a number, a text and a true or false variable, as pseudocode with a trace-table (MS-ALG-PS-01); respond day 10. Tools: our own editor (text view, blocks view, step-through trace; job: run and step the anchor and the student's build). Curio Class writes: the anchor quiz and its two-procedure version, the trace-table and design sheets, the converter rate table, two sample answers for each end-of-unit task | First because every later program, and grade 8's project, needs the notional machine and the roles of each construct, and MS-PRO-RD-17 comes after grade 6's procedures (STANDARD.md ordering: MS-PRO-PD-12 before RD-17; VD-16 before PS-01 and RD-17). Reading comes before designing (APPROACH.md order), so the student designs with constructs already traced. Base: archived Year 8 "Introduction to Python programming", all six lessons (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's order (input and output, then arithmetic, then selection, then iteration) inside sessions 1 to 3, zero added days | No student's real name or detail goes into a program: the quiz stores a made-up player name. "x = x + 1" is read as "x becomes x plus one", never as an equation, with a teacher note. Devices stay on the table, cords behind it. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
