docs/courses/computer-science-grade-8/frame.md:251:| 1 | Systems that hold up. *How do we keep a school's systems working, safe and usable for everyone who needs them?* Anchor: a map of an invented school district's network on the board, one link cut, and a fake "reset your password" message on the screen beside it | 13 | g8-network-failure; g8-cyber-attacks; g8-choose-a-system; g8-design-for-all | MS-SYS-SE-33; MS-SYS-IM-36 | Concept area: systems and security. Practices: CT6 (state each user's need as a test condition); CT9 and HCD11 (test a design change, fix, test again); HCD10 (who a system leaves out); IC5 (team roles on the design days); ESR2 (protect users' accounts and data) | Day 1 (unplugged): hook and question; retrieval of g7-internet-resilience (find another path); a new map and a new failure (a cut link, a down server) traced, and the packets' new path redrawn on the packet-strip (~MS-SYS-NT-35, used in a new setting, owned in grade 7). Day 2 (application): redraw the whole map and add one backup link so the failure cuts no one off; justify where. Day 3 (unplugged): retrieval of the triangle (g7-cia-effects); common attacks on cards (a fake message asking for a password, a program hiding inside another, a guessed password, a flood of requests that takes a service down) matched to the side they hurt; the cybersecurity word page. Day 4 (device, toy inbox built into the page): sort messages into safe and fake by named clues; each attack matched to its prevention (updates, backups, a long passphrase, a second check, asking a trusted adult). Day 5 (application): a protection plan for an invented school system, three attacks, each with its side, its prevention and why that prevention fits (MS-SYS-SE-33, evaluate both attacks and preventions). Day 6 (unplugged): retrieval of g7-system-requirements; two systems for a school office compared on the system-diagram against requirements another group wrote, with an energy note and an ethics note. Day 7: a written justification checked line by line against those requirements by a partner (~MS-SYS-HW-30, used in a new setting, owned in grade 6). Day 8 (device, practice sign-up page): retrieval of g7-access-differences; the page tried against four users' stated needs as test conditions (keyboard only, text enlarged to twice its size, read without color, plain words for a reader new to English); the barrier found. Day 9 (team design): each team proposes a design change for the barrier on the system-diagram and a paper mock-up. Day 10: teammates test the change against each stated need and record what still fails. Day 11: revise and retest (MS-SYS-IM-36, improve a design together for different users). Out: no real attack, scan or account, no encryption mechanics (grade 9's g9-security-measures compares measures), no protocol detail (grade 9 and 10), no operating system layer (g9-operating-systems). End-of-unit (day 12): a new map with a new failure to redraw with a backup link; three new attacks, each matched to its side and a prevention and the choice explained (SE-33); a new system with a new barrier: a design change for two stated needs and how the team would test it (IM-36); a short justification against new requirements, recorded for the HW-30 thread; respond day 13. Tools: a toy inbox page (messages built into the page, job: practice spotting a fake message with no real account); a practice sign-up page (job: a barrier to find and a change to test, keyboard and zoom testable). Curio Class writes: the network maps, attack and prevention cards, the word page, the requirements sets, the sign-up page and its printed version, the user-need cards | First because it sets the year's rules for shared devices, accounts and toy security pages before the project and the AI days use them, and because its four rows need only grade 7's systems rows, which open as retrieval. The barrier-and-test days teach testing with users' stated needs, which unit 2's refine-for-users days use on the students' own programs. Base: archived Year 9 "Introduction of cybersecurity" lessons 2, 4 and 6 for days 3 to 5 (Source map); the network, system choice and design days are our own | Candidate: kept, the partial base's social engineering lesson order (how the trick works, then the clue to spot it) inside days 3 and 4, zero added days | Disability: needs are test conditions on cards, never a role a student acts out; talk is about what the page lets a person do, never about a classmate; use the wording the sensitivity read settles from its pack. Online safety (days 3 to 5): no real password, account or message is used or asked for; the teacher page carries health's "If a student tells you something" steps word for word, since a fake-message day can bring up a real one a student or family received. The flood attack is described, never run. Devices: cords behind the table, carried closed. Agent reads: sensitivity (the unit 1 pack in Sensitivity reads first). Facts: the fake-message clues are checked against the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency's "Recognize and Report Phishing" page (cisa.gov/secure-our-world/recognize-and-report-phishing, as of 2026-11-25); the district and its network are invented for practice. |
docs/courses/computer-science-grade-8/frame.md:252:| 2 | Make it, credit it, hand it over. *What does it take to make a program someone else can use, trust and build on?* Anchor: a working word-guess game from "another class" run on the screen: it accepts a guess of the wrong length, shows right letters only in green, and uses a word-picking function with no credit | 15 | g8-text-project; g8-attribution; g8-refine-for-users | MS-PRO-PD-14; MS-PRO-TR-20 | Concept area: programming. Practices: CT8 (design with a flowchart and pseudocode, then build); CT7 (a procedure reused for every guess); CT9 (planned test-table, one fix at a time); IC4 (project milestones on a shared plan); IC5 (driver and navigator, switched at the halfway signal); IC3 (a partner reads and explains the code); ESR2 (credit others' code); HCD11 (refine for use and access) | Day 14: hook and question; retrieval of g7-design-with-variables (a flowchart read back). Day 15 (trace, device or paper): the worked word-guess game (about twenty lines of Python: a list of words, a list of letters guessed, a tries-left variable, a procedure that checks one guess) traced with a trace-table and a list-diagram; retrieval of g7-lists and g7-analyze-code (the role of each line). Day 16 (modify): add words to the list and change the loop that shows the hidden word with blanks; trace again. Day 17 (modify): change the procedure so a guess of the wrong length is refused with a plain message, and the tries rule; recheck the old tests. Day 18 (design, unplugged): each pair designs its own small text game on a word list from a neutral topic (planets, US state capitals, animals, a class-library book) as a flowchart and pseudocode, with its test-table planned before any code; a pair may instead design a number-guess game on the same plan. Days 19 and 20 (make): build in text, list, loop, procedure and score, tests run each day (~MS-ALG-PS-01 and ~MS-PRO-RD-17, used in a new setting, owned in grade 7). Day 21 (unplugged): retrieval of g7-reference-docs; cases of copying a function, remixing a project and using an image, sorted by when credit and when permission are needed; licenses in plain words (free to use with credit, free to use with no conditions, all rights kept); why credit matters to the person who made it and to the next reader. Day 22 (device): the project adds a word-picking function from a supplied page of another person's code with a stated license; students write its credit comment and a two-sentence justification of why credit and permission matter in software (MS-PRO-PD-14). Day 23 (make, draft day): finish the draft, run every test. Day 24: hand-over: retrieval of g7-collab-text's code review checklist; a partner traces the key part on a trace-table and writes what it does, one thing unclear and one question. Day 25 (understanding then test): retrieval of g7-test-refine; the anchor game tried by keyboard alone, enlarged, without color and with its output read aloud line by line, its faults named as a worked example; then two classmates try each project the same way and write usability and accessibility feedback on a form. Day 26 (refine): one change at a time from the feedback, each logged. Day 27: retest with the same two classmates; the change log finished (MS-PRO-TR-20). Day 28: hand-in: final test-table run, rubric self-check, one paragraph on what was tested and changed; scored on the project rubric. Out: no key-value record or structure choice (grade 9's g9-structures-program), no parameters design beyond one procedure (grade 9's g9-optimize-procedures), no modules or libraries (g9-modular), no timed guesses, no student names stored. End-of-unit: the mid-year course-level check (day 29) stands for it, with the project rubric score from day 28. Tools: our own editor (Python text, a step-through trace, stated tests shown as "yes" or "not yet"; job: build, trace and test the game; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md); a supplied code page with a license line. Curio Class writes: the worked game with its trace and bugs, the topic word lists, the word-picking code page and its license line, the credit cases, the feedback form, the rubric, a finished solution for every step | After unit 1, whose stated-needs testing these refine days reuse on students' own programs, and the first programming unit of the year so the project's list, loop and procedure are fresh for unit 4's code samples, which are written against the same game. Attribution sits inside the build, on the day a borrowed function enters, so the credit line is written for a real use. Refine-for-users follows the draft so feedback has a program to act on. Base: archived Year 9 "Python programming with sequences of data" lessons 1 and 5 for days 15, 19, 20 and 23, and Year 8 "Layers of computing systems" lesson 6 (sharing and open source) for day 21 (Source map); grade 7 adapted the sequences unit's lessons 2 to 4 | Candidate: kept, the partial base's live-coding of the worked program before pairs try it, inside day 15, zero added days | Word lists come from neutral topics; no word or prompt asks about a student, a family or a home. No student's name, photo or voice goes into a program (STANDARD.md "Privacy and safety"). Feedback is about the program, never the classmate: the form asks what worked, one barrier met and one suggestion. Devices: cords behind the table. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
docs/courses/computer-science-grade-8/frame.md:253:| 3 | Data that answers back. *Does a hotter day bring more swimmers to the town pool, and how far can a dataset's answer be trusted?* Anchor: sixty days of an invented town pool's daily high temperature and visitor count, and a headline that claims "heat doubles pool crowds" | 12 | g8-relationships; g8-investigation-summary; g8-biased-decisions | MS-DAT-DI-25; MS-DAT-DI-27; MS-DAT-IM-29 | Concept area: data and analysis. Practices: CT6 (pose the investigation's question); CT7 (find the pattern in the scatter); IC3 (written summary, partner feedback); ESR1 (whose decisions bias a dataset, and who is hurt by the result) | Day 31 (unplugged): hook and question; retrieval of g7-sort-filter-groups and g7-viz-choices; twelve days of the table plotted by hand as a scatter plot. Day 32 (device): the whole table in the table-and-scatter tool; the trend described and used to predict visitors on a 31 degree day, and days classified as busy or quiet by a threshold (MS-DAT-DI-25, find a relationship with a tool and use it to predict and to classify). Day 33 (device): two more invented sets, one with no relationship and one where one value falls as the other rises; which variable helps predict, and that a relationship is not a cause. Days 34 and 35 (investigation, device): pairs choose a question of one of three supplied sets (the pool set, a plant-growth-and-light set, a bus-ridership-and-rain set, all invented for practice), find a relationship, make a prediction, and draw the fairest chart (retrieval of g7-viz-choices). Day 36 (understanding): a given summary with an overreaching claim graded against its evidence; the parts of a summary named: question, method, data source, result, limits, possible biases. Day 37 (draft): each student writes the summary of the pair's investigation. Day 38: written partner feedback on a checklist, then revision (MS-DAT-DI-27). Day 39 (unplugged): retrieval of g7-personal-data; one invented dataset (a town's survey about a new park) followed through its stages, collect, clean, label, summarize and train, with a decision at each stage that can bias it (who was asked, which rows were dropped, how a label was chosen). Day 40 (application): each pair changes one stage's decision and redraws the chart, or reruns a toy model, to show how the decision carries into a misleading conclusion or a poor model (MS-DAT-IM-29). Out: no line of best fit by equation or correlation coefficient (math grade 8 fits a line by eye; high school statistics), no simulated data (grade 9's g9-simulate-data), no data dictionary (g9-data-dictionary), no real person's data. End-of-unit (day 41): a new table: find the relationship with the tool (or by hand on the paper path) and make one prediction and one classification (DI-25); a new summary with two faults: name the missing limit and the claim the evidence does not support (DI-27), with day 38's revised summary scored on its checklist; a new dataset's stages: place two decisions that would bias it and say what each does to the result (IM-29); respond day 42. Tools: a table-and-scatter tool (a table, a scatter, a trend line drawn by eye, a filter; job: find and use the relationship; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md). Curio Class writes: the four invented data sets with "invented for practice" on each, the overreaching summary, the stage cards for the park survey, the summary checklist | After math grade 8 teaches scatter plots and association (see Cross-subject), so the tool days use a scatter the students already read. Before unit 4 because its biased-decisions row ends in a "poor model", which unit 4's classifier then tests, and because a model's test results are a data table read with this unit's moves. The summary follows the investigation so it summarizes a process the student ran. Base: archived Year 9 "Data science" lessons 1 to 6 (Source map), its global data sets replaced by invented ones | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "clean it up" step as the clean stage of day 39, zero added days | All data is invented and about things (temperature, visitors, plants, buses); the park survey's people are invented and unnamed. No class data about students, health or homes. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
docs/courses/computer-science-grade-8/frame.md:254:| 4 | AI under test. *When should we trust what a computer produces, and how do we check it?* Anchor: a classifier that sorts drawings of fruit as ripe or not ripe gets most of them right and almost every green ripe fruit wrong | 12 | g8-ml-evaluate; g8-modify-for-bias; g8-ai-tool-day; g8-evaluate-ai-code | MS-ALG-ML-08; MS-ALG-IM-11; MS-ALG-PS-05; MS-PRO-RD-18 | Concept area: algorithms and design (with machine learning and programming). Practices: CT9 (test a model or a code sample against a test-table); CT8 (judge a generated output against the problem); ESR1 (who a rule or a model treats worse); HCD12 (a model card keeps users informed); IC3 (written verdicts with reasons) | Day 43: hook and question; retrieval of g7-improve-training-data (accuracy measured on a test set). Day 44 (device, classifier test page): the model's test results as a table: accuracy, which kind of example is wrong most, a false positive and a false negative; a model is like a function, an input goes in and an output comes out, with errors. Day 45 (device): a feature that explains the errors (color stands in for ripeness), one thing the model cannot do, and where it should not be used (MS-ALG-ML-08, its features and its limits). Day 46 (application): a short model card supplied in the page (what it was trained on, where to use it and not, the computing it needs, its risks) checked against the test results, and one line the card should add. Day 47 (unplugged): retrieval of g7-examine-algorithm-harm; a school club sign-up lottery whose rule favors the first to arrive traced on ten invented cases; the harm named (students who arrive by late bus are never picked). Day 48 (modify): the rule changed in pseudocode or text and retested on the same ten cases, showing the change and checking it does not make a new harm (MS-ALG-IM-11). Day 49: retrieval of g7-pick-approach; students propose a prompt for a small programming problem (a function that counts how many letters of a guess are in the hidden word); the teacher runs it on one screen with a school-approved tool and no student accounts, or opens Curio Class's saved outputs; the class judges the output against the problem and says what a person must still check (MS-ALG-PS-05). Day 50 (trace): a saved generated sample for a requirement from the unit 2 game (keep a list of past scores and show the best) traced with a test-table; the first wrong line named as a worked example. Day 51 (device): four saved samples for the same requirement, some right and some flawed (one calls a function that does not exist); each run on the test-table and judged for accuracy and usability, first wrong line named. Day 52 (application): a written verdict on which sample a pair would put into its game project, and one change that makes it clearer (MS-PRO-RD-18, judge generated code for a programming project). Out: no training a model (grade 7 trained one), no AI algorithm types (grade 9's g9-ai-tour), no probability of outputs (grade 9's g9-det-vs-prob), no live generated-text tool used by a student, no account. End-of-unit (day 53): a new test-results table and model card: two features, one limit, one place it should not be used (ML-08); a new rule with a harm: modify it and show the retest (IM-11); a new saved output judged against its problem (PS-05); a new generated sample: first wrong line, a test that catches it, and the fix (RD-18); respond day 54. Tools: a classifier test page (a model built into the page, its test set and results; job: read accuracy and errors by kind; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md); our own editor (job: run the saved samples on the test-table); on day 49 only, a school-approved tool on the teacher's screen or the saved outputs. Curio Class writes: the fruit drawings and test set, the model card, the lottery cases, the prompt task and the saved outputs, the four code samples with a key naming each flaw | After unit 3, whose biased-decision stages explain why a model fails on some examples, and after math grade 8's function unit (see Cross-subject). After unit 2, so the generated code samples are judged against the students' own game project, which is what MS-PRO-RD-18 asks. Model testing comes before the generated-output days so students meet "a computer's output can be wrong in patterned ways" on a model they can see before they judge a tool's code. Our own (the partial base's key stage 3 has no AI unit) | Candidate: kept, a second lottery rule with a different harm as the respond day's new form, zero added days | Fruit drawings, never faces or people, so no model sorts people. The lottery's cases are invented; talk is about the rule, never about which families have cars or time. Day 49: no student types into or makes an account with a tool (STANDARD.md "Generated-text tools"); a school with no approved tool uses the saved outputs and the course marks MS-ALG-PS-05 part met. No page says how Curio Class was made (STANDARD.md "The word AI"). Facts: the model card's parts follow Mitchell and others, "Model Cards for Model Reporting", 2019 (arxiv.org/abs/1810.03993), teacher background only; the model and its results are invented for practice. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
docs/courses/computer-science-grade-8/frame.md:255:| 5 | New technology, our town. *How does a new technology change life in a town like ours, and who decides?* Anchor: two invented families book the same video doctor visit: one joins in a click, the other has a slow connection, one shared phone and an app only in English | 8 | g8-emerging-design; g8-history-case; g8-emerging-debate; g8-career-sketch | MS-SOC-ET-41; MS-SOC-ET-42 | Concept area: computing and society. Practices: HCD10 (who a design decision serves); ESR1 (weigh who gains and who is harmed); IC3 (claim, backing, rebuttal); IC5 (debate teams with roles) | Day 55 (unplugged): hook and question; retrieval of g7-when-to-use-ai; the video visit case: a federal survey report on telehealth use in 2021 found video visits less common, and phone-only visits more common, among older adults, people with lower incomes and some other groups; which design decisions (video required or a phone option, the bandwidth the app needs, the languages it offers, who tested it) changed the experience for which communities, with two views and the report as the dated source. Day 56 (application): a second case, invented for practice: a route tool that does not know a new rural road; students write how the design decision changes the experience for town and rural users and who should decide the fix (MS-SOC-ET-41). Day 57 (unplugged): retrieval of g7-impact-history; the history case: credit records kept by private bureaus before 1970, when people could not see or correct their files, and the Fair Credit Reporting Act of 1970, which gave them that right; what a rule could have done earlier, added to the class timeline with the later technologies it applies to (~MS-SOC-HI-39, used in a new setting, owned in grade 7). Day 58: debate preparation: the question "Should our town welcome a large data center?" (the town invented for practice; the national energy figures from the federal report below); two sides prepared from a supplied packet, each claim tied to a dated source, roles assigned (opening, evidence, rebuttal, summary). Day 59: the structured debate on the social, cultural and environmental effects (water, power, jobs, land, the town's character), every claim backed, rebuttals on the other side's evidence (MS-SOC-ET-42, debate effects on a local community; ~MS-SOC-HU-43, used in a new setting, owned in grade 6). Day 60: each student writes their own claim, its backing and a rebuttal to the strongest point against it. Day 61 (unplugged): retrieval of g7-automation-work; each student sketches one computing career path of their own interest, its skills, its training and how automation may change it, shared at the level of ideas (~MS-SOC-CE-44, used in a new setting, owned in grade 6). Day 62: the year's look-back: each student places one idea from each unit on a one-page map of the five concept areas and writes the question they would take to grade 9. Out: no company or product named in student or teacher copy, no vote that settles a real town question, no policy writing (high school), no technical comparison with the established technology (grade 9's g9-emerging-differences). End-of-unit: the year-end course-level check (day 63) stands for it. Tools: none (all unplugged). Curio Class writes: the two saved transcripts, the route tool case, the credit records case card, the debate packet with two sides and their sources, the career sketch page | Last because its rows gather the year: unit 1's systems, unit 3's data and unit 4's models are the evidence a debate on a new technology needs, and the history case lends the debate its question of what a rule can do. Debate follows ELA grade 8's discussion unit (see Cross-subject). The career sketch closes the middle school career thread and hands it to grade 9. Our own (the partial base has no society unit) | Candidate: dropped, a visit by a town planner; the packet carries both sides and a visit needs a school's own arrangement, zero days | Video visits (day 55): the case is about how a service was designed and who could use it, never about a student's or family's health, internet or devices; no student says what their home has; use the wording the sensitivity read settles. Debate (days 58 to 60): two sides, neither declared right; students may argue the side they did not choose, and a student whose family works in power, water or technology is not asked about it. Credit records (day 57): about the rule and the records, never a family's debts or credit. Career sketch (day 61): no question about a family member's job; invented, public and trusted-adult options as in career education's rule (Desk q-fix-career-1); automation can touch a family's work, so the teacher note says so (APPROACH.md, "Work and automation"). Facts: the video visit figures come from the Office of the Assistant Secretary for Planning and Evaluation's 2022 report on national telehealth use in 2021 (aspe.hhs.gov, as of its publication date), checked at source when the day is written; the credit records case rests on the Federal Trade Commission's page on the Fair Credit Reporting Act (ftc.gov/legal-library/browse/statutes/fair-credit-reporting-act); the data center figures come from Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory's "2024 United States Data Center Energy Usage Report" for the Department of Energy (December 2024, as of that date). Agent reads: sensitivity (the unit 5 sources in Sensitivity reads first). |
